ion fails, use the fallible-allocator technique in `tests/HARNESS.md`: a general context whose malloc fails on the Nth call, sweeping N, with a SEPARATE counter per library, asserting C and Rust return the same code for every N.\n\nAdditionally assert the C's return value equals what `ERRORS.md` documents for that row. If it does NOT, the row was mis-derived — **the C is ground truth**: keep the C-vs-Rust assertion and correct that row's `expected C result` cell in `ERRORS.md`, reporting it. Never change `c_src/`.\n\nFor rows genuinely unreachable in this build (the table flags some), still add an entry carrying the row number with a comment explaining why, plus an assertion that C and Rust agree on the nearest reachable input.\n\n## Rules\n- Fix `src/*.rs` if (and only if) you find a genuine C-vs-Rust divergence; rebuild with `cargo build --release`. Report every such fix precisely. Do NOT touch `c_src/`.\n- Do NOT weaken or delete an assertion to make a failure go away.\n- Run `timeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_c_substitute -- --test-threads=1` and iterate until it passes.\n- If the process dies with no message, the Rust `.so` panicked (`panic = \"abort\"`); isolate with `--exact <testname>`.\n\n## Final report must state\n- the exact command you ran and its final PASS output,\n- the sorted unique set of covered row numbers and confirmation it is exactly 324..379,\n- every `src/*.rs` fix you made (file, what was wrong, what the C does),\n- every `ERRORS.md` cell you corrected and why.","uuid":"dc67321d-92ae-426e-a1ff-90cb66006eb6","session_id":"cde48455-d5ce-4cd5-a0b0-2154082e5ad7"}
