eld on each case — that EXACT spelling is required, a coverage script greps for it.\n\nArea-specific guidance:\n\n- **`pcre2_substring.c` (380-412)** — the `-49 NOSUBSTRING` / `-50 NOUNIQUESUBSTRING` / `-54 UNAVAILABLE` / `-55 UNSET` / `-48 NOMEMORY` triad repeated across every accessor: `substring_length_bynumber`, `substring_copy_bynumber`, `substring_get_bynumber`, the three `*_byname` variants, `substring_nametable_scan`, `substring_number_from_name`, `substring_list_get`. Cover: group number above the capture count, group number above what the ovector could hold, unset group, name that does not exist, DUPNAMES name that maps to several groups, a match_data whose last match FAILED, a match_data never used for a match, buffer exactly one byte too small, buffer of size 0, NULL out-pointers. Note `pcre2_substring_free_8(NULL)` and `pcre2_substring_list_free_8(NULL)` must both be safe no-ops.\n- **`pcre2_match_data.c` (413-418)** — `pcre2_match_data_create_8` with oveccount 0 / 1 / 65535 / 65536 / huge, allocation failure, `pcre2_match_data_create_from_pattern_8` with a NULL code, and the `pcre2_get_*` accessors on a never-matched match_data.\n- **`pcre2_pattern_info.c` (419-436)** — a NULL code, a NULL `where` (the documented size-query form) for each `what`, EVERY `what` value from 0..=26 **plus out-of-range values 27, 28, 100, 0xFFFFFFFF** (`PCRE2_ERROR_BADOPTION` -34), and `PCRE2_ERROR_UNSET` for `FIRSTBITMAP`/`JITSIZE`-style unavailable items. Also corrupt magic number / wrong mode.\n- **`pcre2_context.c` (437-454)** — out-of-range enum arguments to `pcre2_set_newline_8` (valid set 1..6; test 0, 7, 99, 0xFFFFFFFF), `pcre2_set_bsr_8` (valid 1..2; test 0, 3, 99), `pcre2_set_optimize_8` (enumerate the accepted directive values from the C and test several rejected ones), and the `PCRE2_ERROR_BADDATA` returns. Also `pcre2_general_context_create_8` with a NULL malloc or NULL free, and every `*_context_copy_8` under allocation failure.\n- **`pcre2_config.c` (455-459)** — every `what` 0..=16 plus out-of-range (17, 100, 0xFFFFFFFF) with and without a buffer.\n- **`pcre2_serialize.c` (460-488)** — this needs byte-patching a real serialized stream. Encode a couple of patterns, then for each row corrupt the documented field at the documented offset (the table gives exact offsets: magic@0, version@4, config@8, number_of_codes@12, then tables, then per-code blocksize/magic) and assert both libraries return the same code. Also: `number_of_codes <= 0` from the caller, NULL arguments, `pcre2_serialize_get_number_of_codes_8` on a corrupt stream, `pcre2_serialize_free_8(NULL)`, encoding a code list containing a NULL, encoding codes with MIXED character tables (`PCRE2_ERROR_MIXEDTABLES` -30) — build that by compiling one pattern with the default tables and another with `pcre2_maketables` output (remember tables are BORROWED, keep them alive).\n- **`pcre2_error.c` (489-494)** — `pcre2_get_error_message_8` with a zero-size buffer, a buffer one byte too small, an unknown error number, and error number 0.\n- **`pcre2_convert.c` (495-516)** — `PCRE2_ERROR_CONVERT_SYNTAX` (-64) for each malformed glob/POSIX construct, mutually exclusive / missing conversion-type option bits (`PCRE2_ERROR_BADOPTION`), invalid UTF-8 input with and without `PCRE2_CONVERT_NO_UTF_CHECK`, buffer too small, NULL arguments, and out-of-range values to `pcre2_set_glob_separator_8` / `pcre2_set_glob_escape_8`.\n- **`pcre2_valid_utf.c` (517-537)** — one row per `PCRE2_ERROR_UTF8_ERR1..ERR21`. Each row gives the exact triggering byte sequence. Call `_pcre2_valid_utf_8` directly AND reach the same error through `pcre2_compile_8` (invalid UTF pattern) and `pcre2_match_8` (invalid UTF subject); assert the code AND the `erroroffset`/`startchar` match in every case.\n- **rows 538-542** (`Other files — no rejection paths`) — assert the claim: those entry points accept the boundary inputs the row names and behave identically (e.g. NULL-tolerant free functions, and the pure helpers).\n\nFor rows reachable only when an allocation fails, use the fallible-allocator technique in `tests/HARNESS.md`: a general context whose malloc fails on the Nth call, sweeping N, with a SEPARATE counter per library, asserting C and Rust return the same code for every N.\n\nAdditionally assert the C's return value equals what `ERRORS.md` documents for that row. If it does NOT, the row was mis-derived — **the C is ground truth**: keep the C-vs-Rust assertion and correct that row's `expected C result` cell in `ERRORS.md`, reporting it. Never change `c_src/`.\n\n## Rules\n- Fix `src/*.rs` if (and only if) you find a genuine C-vs-Rust divergence; rebuild with `cargo build --release`. Report every such fix precisely. Do NOT touch `c_src/`.\n- Do NOT weaken or delete an assertion to make a failure go away.\n- Run `timeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_c_api -- --test-threads=1` (and `--test phase_c_api2` if you split) and iterate until it passes.\n- If the process dies with no message, the Rust `.so` panicked (`panic = \"abort\"`); isolate with `--exact <testname>`.\n\n## Final report must state\n- the exact command(s) you ran and the final PASS output,\n- the sorted unique set of covered row numbers and confirmation it is exactly 380..542,\n- every `src/*.rs` fix you made (file, what was wrong, what the C does),\n- every `ERRORS.md` cell you corrected and why."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5272,"cache_read_input_tokens":308526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5272,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cde48455-d5ce-4cd5-a0b0-2154082e5ad7","uuid":"2c69a2d6-c64b-4e1d-93b9-f905bbc8fc7b","timestamp":"2026-08-23T10:44:22.684Z"}
