{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NkkwkCX4atUvzVrFtmQb7o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential-test harness briefing\n2\t\n3\tRead `tests/common/mod.rs` first, then skim `tests/phase_b_api.rs` for style.\n4\t\n5\t## Ground rules\n6\t\n7\t1. **The C is ALWAYS correct.** If C and Rust differ, fix `src/*.rs`, never\n8\t   `c_src/`. Never weaken an assertion to make a real divergence pass.\n9\t2. Both libraries are loaded via `libloading` from their `.so`. NEVER call the\n10\t   Rust crate directly. `common::pair()` returns `&'static Pair { c: Api, r: Api }`.\n11\t3. Build/run with `--release`: the harness prefers `target/release/libpcre2.so`,\n12\t   and the release profile is the one with `overflow-checks = false`\n13\t   (matching C). Rebuild with `cargo build --release` after touching `src/`.\n14\t4. The Rust `.so` is built with `panic = \"abort\"`, so a panic inside it kills\n15\t   the whole test process (SIGABRT/SIGSEGV with no message). If that happens,\n16\t   narrow down with `-- --exact --test-threads=1 <testname>`.\n17\t5. Use `timeout 600 cargo test --release ...`. Keep individual tests fast.\n18\t\n19\t## Harness API (`tests/common/mod.rs`)\n20\t\n21\t- `pair() -> &'static Pair` — `p.c` (C) and `p.r` (Rust), both `Api`.\n22\t- `Api` has one field per exported function, already resolved to a raw\n23\t  `extern \"C\"` fn pointer. Public ones are named without the `pcre2_`/`_8`\n24\t  affixes (`compile`, `do_match`, `dfa_match`, `substitute`, `pattern_info`,\n25\t  `match_data_create`, `config`, `get_error_message`, `serialize_encode`, ...);\n26\t  the exported internals are prefixed `p_` (`p_valid_utf`, `p_ord2utf`,\n27\t  `p_xclass`, `p_study`, ...). Grep the `def_api! { ... }` block for the exact\n28\t  field name and signature.\n29\t- `Api::data(&self, sym) -> *const u8` — address of an exported data symbol.\n30\t- `Diffs` — failure collector. `d.eq(&tag, c_value, rust_value)` records a\n31\t  divergence instead of aborting on the first one; `d.finish(\"row description\")`\n32\t  panics with a summary if any were recorded. Prefer this over bare\n33\t  `assert_eq!` when looping over many inputs.\n34\t- `assert_code_eq(a, b, ctx)` — byte-for-byte comparison of two compiled\n35\t  patterns (header fields + name table + bytecode).\n36\t  `assert_code_eq_masked(a, b, allow_flags, ctx)` permits specific `flags` bits\n37\t  to differ (only `PCRE2_DEREF_TABLES` legitimately does, after\n38\t  `pcre2_serialize_decode`).\n39\t- `read_match_out(api, md, rc) -> MatchOut` for `pcre2_match_8`, and\n40\t  `read_match_out_of(api, md, rc, Engine::Dfa)` for `pcre2_dfa_match_8`.\n41\t  These read ONLY the fields the C defines for that `rc` — use them rather than\n42\t  reading the ovector yourself.\n43\t- `Rng::new(seed)` — deterministic SplitMix64: `below(n)`, `range(lo,hi)`,\n44\t  `byte()`, `chance(n)`, `pick(&[..])`, `pick_bytes(&[&[u8]])`.\n45\t- `gen_ascii`, `gen_utf8`, `gen_raw` — random subject generators.\n46\t- `PATTERNS`, `SUBJECTS` — shared corpora.\n47\t- `show(&[u8]) -> String` — escaped rendering for messages.\n48\t- All `PCRE2_*` option/info/config/error constants are re-exported.\n49\t- `bytecode_ptr(code)`, `code_blocksize(code)`, `RealCodeHead` — for the\n50\t  internals that consume compiled bytecode.\n51\t\n52\t## Pitfalls already hit (do not repeat)\n53\t\n54\t- **`PCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED` needs a real NUL-terminated buffer.** `s.as_bytes()`\n55\t  is not NUL-terminated; push a `0` first.\n56\t- **Character tables are BORROWED.** `pcre2_compile` stores the pointer\n57\t  (`re->tables = tables`), which is why `pcre2_code_copy_with_tables` exists.\n58\t  Tables from `pcre2_maketables` must outlive every code compiled against them.\n59\t- **`pcre2_match_data_create` does not initialise `mark`, `startchar` or the\n60\t  ovector.** Only a documented prefix/subset is written per return code. Never\n61\t  compare beyond it (that is what `read_match_out` is for).\n62\t- **`pcre2_callout_block`, `pcre2_callout_enumerate_block` and\n63\t  `pcre2_substitute_callout_block` are three DIFFERENT layouts.** Copy the exact\n64\t  field order from `c_src/include/pcre2.h`; watch the padding after a leading\n65\t  `uint32_t version`.\n66\t- Some rows describe inputs that are out-of-bounds in the C itself (e.g.\n67\t  `_pcre2_compile_get_hash_from_name8` with `length == 0` reads `name[-1]`).\n68\t  Those are not comparable observables — skip them with a comment saying why,\n69\t  rather than crashing the harness.\n70\t\n71\t## Testing malloc-failure rows\n72\t\n73\t`ERRORS.md` has rows reachable only when an allocation fails. Test them with a\n74\tcounting allocator installed through a general context: fail the Nth `malloc`\n75\tfor N = 0, 1, 2, ... and assert C and Rust return the SAME code for each N.\n76\t`phase_b_api.rs::custom_allocator_identical` already proves the two libraries\n77\tmake the identical sequence of allocation requests, so this is well-defined.\n78\tSketch:\n79\t\n80\t```rust\n81\tstatic mut BUDGET: i64 = -1;              // -1 = unlimited\n82\tunsafe extern \"C\" fn fallible_malloc(n: usize, _d: *mut c_void) -> *mut c_void {\n83\t    let b = &mut *ptr::addr_of_mut!(BUDGET);\n84\t    if *b == 0 { return ptr::null_mut(); }\n85\t    if *b > 0 { *b -= 1; }\n86\t    /* real allocation */\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\tUse a SEPARATE counter per library so the two runs cannot interfere, and reset\n90\tbefore each call.\n91\t\n92\t## Required annotation\n93\t\n94\tEvery test case must record which `ERRORS.md` row(s) it covers, in a field\n95\tliterally spelled `rows:` holding a slice of row numbers, e.g.\n96\t\n97\t```rust\n98\tstruct Case { rows: &'static [u32], pat: &'static str, /* ... */ }\n99\tconst CASES: &[Case] = &[\n100\t    Case { rows: &[32], pat: \"a\\\\\", expect: 101 },\n101\t    Case { rows: &[33], pat: \"a\\\\c\", expect: 102 },\n102\t];\n103\t```\n104\t\n105\tA coverage script greps `rows: &[...]` out of every `tests/phase_c_*.rs`, so the\n106\tspelling matters. If a row is genuinely unreachable in this build, still add an\n107\tentry for it with a comment explaining why and an assertion that C and Rust\n108\tagree on whatever the nearest reachable input does.\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Qz7FB2Krnwsnf7BJpPHXu5","session_id":"cde48455-d5ce-4cd5-a0b0-2154082e5ad7","uuid":"d3d65789-bd02-4e78-bbe1-04e043f8072f","timestamp":"2026-08-23T10:49:31.958Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"CONFIGS rows 407-444 low-level sign-off"}
