_match_8` never assigns `startchar` on NOMATCH, for instance). Do NOT read the ovector yourself.\n- For `pcre2_substitute_8` compare the return code, the value written back through `outlengthptr`, and the FULL output buffer including the bytes past the written region (so a buffer overrun is caught).\n\nMake sure each of these is genuinely exercised:\n\n**dfa_match (294-321)**\n- `PCRE2_DFA_SHORTEST`, and the multiple-match-ends semantics: one start position can yield many end offsets, so the ovector holds pairs of *ends* and the count latches to 0 once it overflows — sweep ovector sizes across that boundary.\n- `PCRE2_DFA_RESTART`: it forces `anchored`, disables `firstline` and every start optimization, and requires the identical `wscount`. Drive a genuine partial match first, then restart with the same and with a different workspace size, and compare.\n- workspace sizing: sweep `wscount` around the `(wscount-2)/3` sanity bound and the real `(wscount-2)/6` capacity so the exact crossover is compared.\n- `PARTIAL_SOFT` / `PARTIAL_HARD`, `NOTBOL` / `NOTEOL` / `NOTEMPTY` / `NOTEMPTY_ATSTART`, `ANCHORED` / `ENDANCHORED`, `NO_START_OPTIMIZE`, `COPY_MATCHED_SUBJECT`.\n- UTF and `PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF` subjects, newline conventions, `\\R` conventions.\n- the recursion/condition/callout arms the DFA does support (callouts via `pcre2_set_callout_8` — compare the whole callout sequence), and `pcre2_next_match_8` iterating the recorded ends to exhaustion.\n- match/depth limits via a match context, sweeping the limit across the crossover point.\n\n**substitute (322-351)**\n- every `PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_*` option and their combinations: GLOBAL, EXTENDED, LITERAL, UNSET_EMPTY, UNKNOWN_UNSET, OVERFLOW_LENGTH, REPLACEMENT_ONLY, MATCHED.\n- the replacement mini-language: `$0`/`$1`/`${n}`/`${name}`/`$*MARK`, `\\1`, the case operators `\\U \\L \\u \\l \\E`, and the EXTENDED conditionals `${n:-default}` / `${n:+yes:no}`.\n- the documented two-call sizing protocol: call with a zero-length